meta {
  name: 01 Get me
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.centerUser.emailVerified: eq true
  res.body.onboarding.complete: isDefined
}

docs {
  ## The onboarding block

  Every response carries it:

      "onboarding": { "complete": false, "missing": ["country", "city", "phone"] }

  This is what the dashboard renders its checklist from. It is DERIVED on
  every read, never stored — there is no flag to keep true and therefore none
  that can be stale. Fill the three fields via `02 Patch me` and it becomes
  `{ "complete": true, "missing": [] }` on the very next request, with no job
  to wait for.

  `missing` lists exactly the absent fields, in a stable order, so a client
  never holds a rival copy of the rules: a fourth required field added on the
  server updates every checklist with no frontend change.

  Worth checking by hand: set a field to whitespace (`"city": "   "`) via
  `02 Patch me`. It must still count as missing. Spaces are not an answer.

  The same three fields gate payment — see
  `Center Payments / 02 Create payment` — and that gate reads this same
  derivation, so the checklist and the refusal can never disagree.

  Checks worth doing by hand:

  - no Authorization header      -> 401
  - a malformed or expired token -> 401
  - a STUDENT access token       -> 401, the verifier refuses it outright
  - after logout                 -> 401

  The response must never contain password_hash, email_verification_token, or
  password_reset_token. It is built field by field rather than spread from the
  row, so a new column cannot leak here by default — confirm that holds.
}
